<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Revolt of the Comuneros (New Granada)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1781">1781</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Revolt of the Comuneros was a popular uprising in the Viceroyalty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> in 1781, which began at Socorro against new taxes and royal monopolies imposed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon reforms</a>. Some 20,000 rebels marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> and won concessions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulations-of-the-ottoman-empire">Capitulations</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zipaquira">Zipaquirá</a>, but the authorities later revoked them and executed the leader José Antonio Galán in 1782.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> on 21 June 1779. Its largest effort went into the Great Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>, which consumed money and men for years, and the heavy taxes and so-called voluntary donations raised to pay for the war caused unrest across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> the new levies came with higher prices for tobacco and brandy. They belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon Reforms</a>, through which Spain sought to make its colonies more profitable by curbing tax evasion, creating trading companies and royal monopolies, and tightening control over colonial government. Residents had already protested against crown policies, at times violently, between 1740 and 1779.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 16 March 1781, in Socorro, the grocer Manuela Beltrán tore down the posted edicts announcing the new taxes. Similar protests broke out in other towns, where residents elected committees known as el común to lead the movement. The rebels united under Juan Francisco Berbeo, a member of the Criollo elite, whose support helped bring together people of different social classes. After defeating troops sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>, the rebel army reached a town just north of the capital, where Spanish officials agreed to sign a document setting out the rebels' conditions and complaints.</p><p>Once the rebels had dispersed, the officials repudiated the agreement as having been forced on them, and reinforcements were sent to enforce the taxes. José Antonio Galán carried on with a small band, including José Manuel Ortiz Manosalvas, but they were soon defeated and executed, while other leaders were sentenced to life imprisonment for treason.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The revolt, which lasted from March to October 1781, inspired similar risings as far as Mérida and Timotes, now in Venezuela, which ended the same way. Barinas, which defeated the Comuneros of the Venezuelan Andes, was granted its coat of arms by King Carlos IV in 1790. In the late nineteenth century the historian Manuel Briceño presented the revolt as a forerunner of independence. Others disagree: Brian Hamnett, reviewing John Leddy Phelan's study, argued that the rebels sought only to reverse the reforms, not independence or self-government.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Revolt of the Comuneros (New Granada)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Comunero revolt</b> of 1781 opposed new taxes.</blockquote><a href="/state/viceroyalty-of-new-granada">Viceroyalty of New Granada</a></li></ul></section>
